Hi Frank,

in reply to the one-year-old discussion about boxplots versus
box-percentile-plots:

I have just recently tried bpplots for the first time and
rejected them after a few trials for being very counter-intuitive:

plot a roughly uniform distribution as a bpplot, show the result to
someone, explaining what the plot means and ask him or her to
sketch the density function.
I find (and bet that most others will fall into the same trap)
that the bpplot suggests far higher density near the median
than is actually the case. (A uniform distribution results in a
diamond-shaped bpplot.)

I'll stick with ordinary boxplots plus optional overlaid scatterplots.

What do you think?

  Lutz

