Message-ID: <005401be237e$41d3bec0$5a6c8f80@fharrell.biostat.virginia.edu>

I agree with your assessment Lutz, so far.  You might also look at
"violin plots", whose shapes are a little more intuitive, being
based on informal density estimates:

@Article{hin98vio,
  author =    {Hintze, Jerry L. and Nelson, Ray D.},
  title =    {Violin plots: {A} box plot--density trace synergism},
  journal =  The American Statistician,
  year =    1998,
  volume =   52,
  pages =   {181-184},
  annote =   {box plot;density;graphics;depends on choice of bin
                  width for estimating density trace}
}

I still like the "semi-enhanced" box plot that results from showing 9 quantiles 
instead of 3; 9 is the default in panel.bpplot in Hmisc.  This shows the median 
(and
the mean, using a dot) and the ranges containing 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, and 0.90
of the data.

One slightly helpful trick is, for the full box-percentile plot, to add a bpplot
showing the shape for a sample of normally distributed measurements with
the mean and variance equal to the mean and variance of the overall data.

I am turning more lately to ECDFs, for which Hmisc has a
new trellis function, to allow for multi-panel ECDFs with optional superposition
of groups within a panel.  -Frank Harrell

